  "evidence": "- TEXT_D: Prioritises NHS access and staffing (8,000 doctors, 12,000 nurses; faster diagnosis; free personal care), scrapping tuition fees and cutting class sizes, 10,000 extra police and a UK Border Force, fairer taxes with only one targeted rise (50% over £100k) and replacing Council Tax with Local Income Tax to cut typical household bills, affordable housing pledges, and pragmatic green policies (carbon tax, 20% renewables by 2020) alongside mainstream positions on defence and Europe.\n- TEXT_C: Emphasises radical fiscal changes (50% tax over £50k, 60% over £100k; abolish National Insurance; replace VAT with eco-taxes; Land Value Tax), sweeping nationalisations and a universal Citizen’s Income, major defence cuts and decommissioning nuclear weapons, strong restrictions on aviation and higher motoring costs, and opposition to the proposed EU constitution.",
  "reasoning": "TEXT_D aligns with broadly expressed voter priorities in the mid-2000s—NHS, education, crime/immigration, and household taxes—offering incremental, costed changes and mainstream stances. TEXT_C advances far more radical shifts in taxation, defence, and transport that are less consistent with the typical preferences of the wider British electorate.",
  "confidence": "high",
  "winner": "TEXT_D"
}
